In TypeScript, **method overriding** is a mechanism where a subclass provides a new implementation for a method already defined in its parent class. This allows the subclass to inherit the behavior of the parent class while modifying it to suit its own needs.

To override a method in TypeScript, the method signature in the subclass must exactly match the signature of the method in the parent class.

ts
class Animal {
  makeSound(): void {
    console.log('Making animal sound');
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  makeSound(): void {
    console.log('Bark');
  }
}

let animal: Animal;

animal = new Dog();
animal.makeSound(); // Output: Bark


In this example, the `Dog` class overrides the `makeSound` method defined in the `Animal` class, providing its own implementation. When the `makeSound` method is called on an instance of the `Dog` class, it uses the implementation within the `Dog` class instead of the `Animal` class. 🐶